A||||7||Jncomutt||Rear Calip. Qs.  Prob. answered many times.||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||10-17-2001||01:33 PM||||I did a search, and found a fairly good explanation.  Just for reassurance<P>OK I found out the reason my car pulls right is because the rear brake caliper is not releasing.  I Took my caliper off. cleaned it very well, Removed the slide pins, cleaned em, regreased them, they move easily.  I put the caliper back on, bled it.  It seemed to work fine.  But, when I pulled my ebrake, the caliper got tight but doesn't release all the way.  I'm going to redo the caliper and just not use the ebrake for now, but does this mean the caliper is on last leg and time for a new one???<P>The spring is in place, and the lever fully moves back, just that the piston doesn't retract.  Thanks guys.||||reg||1
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-17-2001||01:37 PM||||whats it like for movement with no cable on it? It might be your ebrake cable getting stiff..?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Voytek||10-17-2001||01:49 PM||||Um, I'm pretty sure it's the e-brake cable that's stuck.  The easy way to check this is to get underneath the car and pull on the cable (in the middle) fairly hard a few times.  If it fixes the problem, you know that's what it is.<P>The e-brake cable consists of 3 cables: front (to the lever), right (right caliper) and left (left caliper).   Start by replacing the one on the caliper that's not releasing.  I seriously doubt it's your caliper; I've gone throught this a couple of times.  A shop even told me my calipers are toast - I went home and replaced the cables.<P>The cables are cheap.  In Canada they run about $22 bucks for left or right (center one is more, I believe).  Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jncomutt||10-17-2001||02:18 PM||||Brand New Cables.  Had em replaced 200 miles ago when they did my clutch.  Damn shop fu*ked something up and on my way home, the (hub?)was glowing bright red.  The shop of course denied knowin what could have happened, And somehow 30 miles just happened to appear on my odometer.  But yea, the cable is new.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jncomutt||10-17-2001||02:21 PM||||Even when the caliper is off the car, and I move the lever, It will push the piston out, but it won't retract.  Is this supposed to be like this and something having to do with the fluid system supposed to bring it back??||||reg||1
Z||000005||Banzai||10-17-2001||04:45 PM||||Did you use your E-brake religiously before??? In my experience, cars with four wheel discs(especially with the screw actuated parking brake) if you don't use the parking brake all the time the screws in caliper will bind over time and the caliper will fail to release.  If all else is new--- your caliper is toast.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||10-17-2001||04:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jncomutt:<BR><B>Even when the caliper is off the car, and I move the lever, It will push the piston out, but it won't retract.  Is this supposed to be like this and something having to do with the fluid system supposed to bring it back??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>That's the way it is supposed to work- keeps the rear brakes adjusted, also when you greased your slider pins I hope you used  brake grease and not regular  grease (regular type destroys the seals after awhile)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jncomutt||10-17-2001||10:26 PM||||Yea, I went out and got a small tube of the special stuff.  I'mm going to try to rebleed the rear brakes.  See if that works.  And yes, I used to use the Ebrake Every time I stopped.||||reg||1
